During the replacement of the direct use of K9 object, we ended up creating a settings manager that defeats the single responsibility principle, risking the creation of another K9 class.

We should step back and change the approach we are taking so far before it is too hard to maintain. Here are a few steps we need to take:

- [ ] Analyze each property inside `GeneralSettings` and verify if it belongs to a group of settings. We could group each of the properties by respecting the grouping we already have in the Settings screen (e.g., Display, Interaction, Notification, etc), and/or by areas (e.g., Account list, Message List, Message Composer, etc.)
- [ ] Split the `GeneralSettingsManager` interface/implementation into smaller interfaces, each one handling the group they are responsible for. The `GeneralSettingsManager` must still implement all other `SettingsPreferenceManager` interfaces.
- [ ] Refactor the `RealGeneralSettingsManager` implementation:
    - [ ] Rename it from `RealGeneralSettingsManager` to `DefaultGeneralSettingsManager`, following our code naming convention
    - [ ] Implement either `GeneralSettingsManager` and the other `SettingsPreferenceManager`s
    - [ ] Receive all the other `SettingsPreferenceManager` interfaces as a composition, so `RealGeneralSettingsManager` can fulfill all the interface requirements without requiring to implementation of their methods by using the [Delegation pattern]([URL]

Notes: 
- Prefer using `PreferenceManager<T>` instead of creating a completely new interface for Preference management
- Avoid creating a `set` method for each Settings property. Instead, you could use an extension function such as:
   ```kotlin
    inline fun <reified T> PreferenceManager<T>.update(updater: (T) -> T) {
        val config = getConfig()
        save(updater(config))
    }
   ```
   And directly calling the `PreferenceManager<T>.update()` to update the settings value, as exemplified next:
   ```kotlin
    class GeneralSettingsDataStore(
        ...
        private val privacySettingsPreference: PreferenceManager<PrivacySettings>,
    ) {
        override fun putBoolean(key: String, value: Boolean) {
            when (key) {
                ....
                "privacy_hide_timezone" -> {
                    skipSaveSettings = true
                    privacySettingsPreference.update { it.copy(isHideTimeZone = value) }
                }
            }
        }
    }
   ```

body:
### Checklist

- [x] I have used the search function to see if someone else has already submitted the same bug report.
- [x] I will describe the problem with as much detail as possible.

### App

K-9 Mail

### App version

10.0

### Where did you get the app from?

Google Play

### Android version

Android 12, One UI Core 4.1

### Device model

Samsung Galaxy A12

### Steps to reproduce

1. Launch Pimlical Android.
2. Go to All Menu Items... > Help & Usage Tips.
3. Tap [Email Tech Support].
4. Tap each of the three "Select File Attachments" check boxes.
5. Tap [Send].
6. Tap [K-9 Mail] at the Android app selection dialog.
7. K-9 Mail opens a compose window, pre-filled by Pimlical with debug information, including the line: "# of Error/Log/Pref Files attached: 8." Yet, only one file is actually attached to the message.

### Expected behavior

K-9 Mail should have attached all eight files that were passed to it.

### Actual behavior

K-9 Mail attached only one file.

### Logs

The following code of Pimlical Android demonstrates how the eight files to be attached were passed to K-9 Mail:

`//
// See if there are any error/log/preference files to attach...
//
ArrayList<Uri> uri = new ArrayList<Uri>();
File pFolder = new File(Prefs.userPath + filePathSeparator);
File[] listOfFiles = pFolder.listFiles();
long sizeOfAllAttachments = 0;
if (listOfFiles != null && listOfFiles.length > 0) {
    for (int i = 0; i < listOfFiles.length; i++) {
        String fName = listOfFiles[i].getName();
        if (listOfFiles[i].isFile()
                && ((itemsSelected.contains(0) && (fName.endsWith(FileSystemPaths.ERRORFILE_EXTENSION_WITH_DOT)
                || fName.equalsIgnoreCase(FileSystemPaths.PIMLICAL_DEBUG_INFO_FILENAME)))
                || (itemsSelected.contains(1) && fName.endsWith(FileSystemPaths.LOGFILE_EXTENSION_WITH_DOT))
                || (itemsSelected.contains(2) && (fName.equalsIgnoreCase("default.dat")
                || fName.equalsIgnoreCase("default.txt")
                || fName.equalsIgnoreCase("systempreferences.dat"))))) {
            File pFile = new File(pFolder.getPath() + filePathSeparator + fName);
            if (pFile.length() > 0) { // Ignore zero-length files here
                if (pFile.length() + sizeOfAllAttachments > MAXIMUM_SIZE_FOR_ALL_ATTACHMENTS) {
                    break;                                  // Must quit now
                }
                uri.add(FileProvider.getUriForFile(mainContext, mainContext.getPackageName() + ".provider", pFile));
                sizeOfAllAttachments += pFile.length();
            }
        }
    }
}
if (uri.size() > 0) {
    info += "# of Error/Log/Pref Files attached: " + uri.size() + "\n";
}
info += "\nEnter, in English below, the Support Issue AND your expertise level (Novice, Intermediate, Expert)\n";
info += "============================\n";
String subject = "Pimlical/Android Direct Technical Support Request";

String actionIntent = Intent.ACTION_SEND;
if (uri.size() > 0) {
    actionIntent = Intent.ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE;
}
Intent emailIntent = new Intent(actionIntent);
//                                                              Intent emailIntent = new Intent(actionIntent, Uri.fromParts("mailto:", "SWSupport@PimlicoSoftware.com", null));
emailIntent.setType("text/plain");
emailIntent.putExtra(Intent.EXTRA_SUBJECT, subject);
emailIntent.putExtra(Intent.EXTRA_EMAIL, new String[]{"SWSupport@PimlicoSoftware.com"});
emailIntent.putExtra(Intent.EXTRA_TEXT, info);
if (uri.size() > 0) {
    emailIntent.addFlags(Intent.FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION);
    emailIntent.putParcelableArrayListExtra(Intent.EXTRA_STREAM, uri);
}
try {
    startActivity(Intent.createChooser(emailIntent, "Send email..."));
} catch (Exception e) {
    ReadWriteCalendar.writeErrorFile(mainContext, "setupHelpDialog()", "Unable to Launch Email App", e);
    MsgBox.msg(mainContext, pimlicalResources.getString(R.string.no_email_title),
            pimlicalResources.getString(R.string.email_tech_support), R.drawable.warningicon);
}
`

